<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>

    <!--
      Pure logic — no Siemens.Engineering, no network, no Portal. The runner talks to an ITransport
      and nothing else, so the whole vector model and every refusal path is testable with no PLC in
      the room. Keeping it net8.0 and off anything that touches Portal means changing the harness
      NEVER triggers the TIA Openness (Path,FileHash) re-approval cycle that a rebuild of
      openness-cli does. STILL TRUE, and verified rather than assumed: no harness .csproj references
      src/converter (zero hits across all 35 of them), and the cross-solution references that DO
      exist — src/device-guard and src/download-feedback — are net8.0 / netstandard2.0 with no
      Siemens dependency of their own.

      *** BUT "DEPENDENCY-FREE" IS NO LONGER THE RULE, AND THIS COMMENT SAID IT WAS. ***
      RULING C2, 2026-08-23: src/harness MAY take a project reference on src/converter. CONVERTER
      ONLY — it does not generalise, and the adjacent src/wave-control decision (a file contract, not
      a project reference) is untouched. Recorded in docs/18-project-workbench.md §3.6 (docs/18:252-272),
      which predicted at :255-258 that a reader would meet the constraint HERE and find no hint of it.
      They now do.

      *** AND THE FINDING THAT MAKES C2 HARDER THAN IT SOUNDS — recorded nowhere else. ***
      There is NO library project to reference. src/converter/Converter/Converter.csproj is
      OutputType=Exe, AssemblyName=converter, and it ProjectReferences ..\..\wave-control\WaveControl.
      So taking C2 up means one of two things, and neither is a one-line edit:

        (a) split a Converter.Core library out — a converter build-layout change while every skill
            invokes bin/Release/converter by path; or
        (b) reference the Exe as it stands — which works (src/harness already ProjectReferences
            src/device-guard, itself OutputType=Exe, from six projects, so this is not novel) but
            pulls WaveControl into the harness's build graph behind it. WaveControl is netstandard2.0
            and references nothing, so the build cost is near zero — the cost is that docs/18 §5 row
            1.3's "verified zero project references between src/harness and src/wave-control" would
            become false transitively, by a route nobody chose.

      Whoever takes C2 up decides between those and says which in the commit. Until then the
      re-approval claim above holds because no such reference exists, not because none may.

      Warnings-as-errors matches the other solutions (audit F-37): there is no CI, so the compiler is
      the only thing that notices drift.
    -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
